<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

    <!--
      The grain-backed half of metering's tests: the sampler run twice over one window, the
      redelivered event that collapses and the different window that does not, the state-based meter
      that bills a resource which exists and is not running, the ledger that cannot be edited, the
      quota lease released on failure, and the sink outage that leaves an hour open rather than
      losing it.

      ⚠ NO Testcontainers AND NO Aspire.Hosting, AND THAT IS THE SAME DEVIATION FROM ADR-018 THAT
      CyberCloud.ResourceManager.Tests RECORDS, OWED BACK ON THE SAME TERMS. docs/plan/02 ADR-018
      wants grain behaviour tested "against a real in-process TestCluster … with real storage
      providers backed by Testcontainers". This project uses Orleans.TestingHost's TestCluster with
      AddMemoryGrainStorage and UseInMemoryReminderService instead, because the environment this was
      built in could not start a container.

      What that costs, stated so it can be paid rather than forgotten:

        - Nothing here proves the state SERIALIZES. In-memory storage keeps the object graph; a real
          provider round-trips it. UsageRollupState.SeenKeys is a Dictionary<string, DateTimeOffset>
          and UsageLedgerState.Entries a List<UsageLedgerEntry>, both `{ get; set; }` for exactly the
          "STJ does not populate a get-only collection" trap — but the assertion that they round-trip
          is owed, and for the ledger it is the assertion that matters most, because a ledger that
          fails to deserialize is a ledger that has silently lost every entry.
        - Nothing here proves the DURABLE tier is durable across a silo restart. Deactivation is used
          to exercise re-activation, which keeps the same in-process store.
        - The reminder tests exercise registration and the drive body, not Redis reminder storage
          (docs/plan/04 § Reminders). Both grains here are IRemindable and both are driven directly.

      Everything else — the idempotency key's behaviour under redelivery, the sampler's window snap,
      the ledger's append-only shape, the rollup's ordering of ledger-then-sink-then-drop, and the
      quota integration — is behaviour of our code and is fully exercised here.
    -->

    <ItemGroup>
        <PackageReference Include="xunit.v3"/>
        <PackageReference Include="Shouldly"/>
        <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.Orleans.TestingHost"/>
        <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.Orleans.Reminders"/>
        <PackageReference Include="Orleans.Multitenant"/>
    </ItemGroup>

    <ItemGroup Label="Global usings">
        <Using Include="Xunit"/>
    </ItemGroup>

    <ItemGroup>
        <ProjectReference Include="../CyberCloud.Metering/CyberCloud.Metering.csproj"/>
        <!--
          The REAL quota grain. docs/plan/22 § Quota's "a reservation in IQuotaGrain before the
          provider is called, released on failure" is already implemented in CyberCloud.Tenancy, and
          QuotaIntegrationTests asserts the integration against it rather than reimplementing a
          reservation to assert against a fake.
        -->
        <ProjectReference Include="../CyberCloud.Tenancy/CyberCloud.Tenancy.csproj"/>
    </ItemGroup>

</Project>
